  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Saturday)\nIssued at 1204 PM CST SUN NOV 22 2026\n\nLow clouds will remain in place for most of the morning, as low-\nlevel moisture remains in place from this stagnant airmass.\nConditions will gradually clear into the afternoon, as a subtle\nridge builds in ahead of the approaching cold front. Moisture\nreturn ahead of this cold front will be very limited, with the\ncold front beginning to take a more E/W shape due to the upper-\nlevel support. Because of the cold front orientation, and lack of\nmoisture, rain probabilities will be best in our NW zones Sunday\nnight. Additional shower development is possible during the day\non Monday south of I-20, but confidence is high that any\nadditional shower activity won't amount to any meaningful\nmeasurable rainfall.\n\nThis first cold front should push through the region during the\nday on Monday, stalling near the coast by Tuesday. Temperatures\nwill drop slightly behind this front, as colder air filters down\nthe Appalachian mountains under a CAD wedge. A much stronger\nsystem will begin to take shape to our west during this same\ntimeframe, as a trough moving through the Plains becomes cut off\nfrom the main upper-level flow. This closed low is expected to\nwork through the region on Wednesday, with increased forcing and\nmoisture resulting in some of the higher rain chances we've\nrecently seen. Widespread rainfall should begin by Wednesday\nmorning, continuing throughout the day and into the overnight\nhours. Most locations will generally see between a half an inch,\nand one inch of rainfall, before this closed low exits the region\ninto Thanksgiving proper. Some lingering showers are possible\nnorth of I-20 into Thursday, but most locations are expected to\nremain dry for Thanksgiving Day. With that being said, much\ncolder temperatures are expected behind this closed low, with\nseasonable to slightly below seasonable temperatures remaining in\nplace into next weekend.\n\n/44/",
